import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const doeCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Randomise', note: 'Assign treatments by chance. Randomisation distributes every hidden factor — soil, mood, genotype — evenly between groups, so differences at the end belong to the treatment.' },
    { math: '2 · Replicate', note: 'Repeat enough times to separate signal from noise. No replication = no error estimate = no conclusion, however dramatic the result looks.' },
    { math: '3 · Block what you can\'t randomise', note: 'Known nuisances (batch, day, field position) get grouped into blocks, and treatments randomised within each. Fisher: "block what you can, randomise what you cannot."' },
    { math: '4 · Factorial thinking', note: 'Vary several factors at once (2×2 design) and you learn interactions too — "fertiliser helps only with irrigation" is invisible to one-factor-at-a-time testing.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/advanced/experimental-design">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Experimental design is <strong>planning the comparison before collecting the data</strong> —
    the discipline Ronald Fisher built at Rothamsted agricultural station in the 1920s and 30s,
    where decades of field trials met new statistics. Its law: the quality of a conclusion is
    decided at design time; no analysis can rescue a confounded experiment.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Fisher's three pillars plus the factorial upgrade" widget="equationStep" config={doeCfg} />

  <h2>Classic designs, real uses</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Randomised controlled trial (RCT):</strong> the medical gold standard — placebo control, double-blinding, pre-registered endpoints.</li>
    <li><strong>A/B test:</strong> the industrial RCT — randomise users to two versions of a page. Same statistics, faster feedback (the data-science ladder covers the pitfalls, like peeking).</li>
    <li><strong>Randomised block / Latin square:</strong> when field position or test-day matters, block it out. Fisher's original farmland problem, still solved the same way.</li>
    <li><strong>Sample-size/power planning:</strong> decide n before starting, from the smallest effect worth detecting. Underpowered studies waste everything — the replication crisis in one sentence.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    When experiments are impossible (smoking causes cancer — you can't randomise), the
    <strong>observational</strong> toolkit takes over: matching, natural experiments, causal
    inference — the expert lesson on this ladder.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Fisher, The Design of Experiments (1935); Box, Hunter & Hunter, Statistics for Experimenters.</p>
</TopicPage>
